html='''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Learned NN selector</title><style>body{font:17px system-ui;max-width:1220px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 20px;line-height:1.55;color:#19283b}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px}th,td{padding:7px;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd}th{background:#edf3f8}img{width:100%}.note{padding:20px;background:#edf3f8;border-radius:10px}h2{margin-top:40px}</style></head><body><h1>Learning when to include a nearest-neighbour explanation</h1><div class="note"><b>Result:</b> the validation-selected gate reaches 79.985% test F1 at fixed50, versus 79.187% for always-NN and 79.555% for the cosine threshold. This is +0.798 and +0.430 percentage points, respectively. Logistic-only gates reach 80.022%, shallow trees 79.887%. The primary result remains the selector chosen on validation, not whichever family looks best on test.</div><img src="learned_gate_f1.png"><h2>What was trained?</h2><p>A separate pooled gate for each original judge and threshold policy, trained across all five backbones and ten datasets. Supervision comes only from validation disagreements: include NN if its original classification is correct, otherwise use control. Unchanged predictions are indifferent and do not train the classifier, but remain in F1 evaluation. The classifier sees pre-judge inputs only: probe probability, neighbour training label and probe prediction, cosine similarity, label/probe agreement, simple interactions, text lengths, lexical overlap, and dataset/backbone identity. It does not see the target gold label, the original judge outputs, or the test-only mechanism annotations.</p><h2>Validation and test separation</h2><ol><li>25,000 validation contexts, representing 17,192 distinct target clusters; no exact target-cluster overlap with the 25,000 test contexts.</li><li>Three grouped cross-validation folds keep repeated versions of the same target together across backbones. Scaling is fitted on training folds only.</li><li>Compare logistic regression with C=0.1 or1 and shallow decision trees with depth3 or5 and minimum50 training examples per leaf.</li><li>For each candidate, select one gate cutoff per judge/backbone/dataset using out-of-fold validation predictions and weighted F1. Always/never-NN endpoints are included; ties prefer fewer NN prompts.</li><li>Select the model per judge/policy using macro validation F1, refit on validation disagreements, and save the frozen choices before testing. Best hyperparameters within each family are also frozen for the family comparisons.</li><li>Apply to test with the original probe/judge thresholds unchanged. The shared tuned sensitivities reuse the three pre-existing anchor selections; their average is a sensitivity summary, not a single deployed threshold.</li></ol><p>k-NN was not tested in this first pass. Logistic regression is a compact inspectable baseline; shallow trees test nonlinear rules without a large model search. This is an exploratory follow-up on a previously inspected test set. Cross-validation scores used to select models and cutoffs are selection scores, not unbiased performance estimates. Point estimates below do not include uncertainty from retraining the gate.</p><h2>Fixed50 test results</h2><p>Metrics are percentages, macro-averaged over the same 500 original judge/backbone/dataset combinations.</p>'''+tab(q[q.policy=='fixed50'][['method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','nn_rate']])+'''<p>The selected gate improves F1 in 213 cells, worsens it in 176 and ties in 111 versus always-NN. Versus cosine, it improves 246, worsens 145 and ties in109. NN content is included for 32.75% of the evaluation population, versus47.02% with always-NN. Referral counts do not change; this removes explanation content from some prompts, not final judge calls.</p><h2>Which judges benefit?</h2><p>Fixed50 test F1, with percentage-point differences in the last two columns.</p>'''+tab(judge.reset_index())+'''<h2>Models selected on validation</h2>'''+tab(choice)+'''<h2>Shared tuned threshold results</h2>'''+tab(q[q.policy=='anchor_average'][['method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','nn_rate']])+'''<p>The learned selector adds only0.036 F1 points over cosine after averaging the three shared tuned settings. The cosine baseline is slightly better for the control-anchor setting. This suggests most of the remaining gain after probe/judge tuning is small; the classifier is not uniformly better across settings.</p><h2>All policy results</h2>'''+tab(q[['policy','method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','nn_rate']])+'''<h2>Four toxicity/content-safety datasets</h2>'''+tab(co[['policy','method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy']])+'''<h2>Reproducibility</h2><p>All7,500 control, always-NN and cosine benchmark rows exactly replay to numerical tolerance. Saved fold assignments, candidate selection scores, thresholds, models, per-case test predictions and checksums are available below. No new LLM calls, no new semantic annotations, and no probe retraining were used. A tuned setting with zero validation disagreements uses a constant gate and is explicitly recorded.</p><ul>'''+''.join('<li><a href="'+x+'">'+x+'</a></li>' for x in ['model_choices.csv','validation_candidates.csv','validation_cutoffs.csv','validation_folds.csv.gz','cell_metrics.csv','summary.csv','deltas.csv','test_predictions.csv.gz','feature_importance.csv','SELECTION_FROZEN.json','COMPLETED.json','learned_gate_f1.pdf'])+'''</ul><p><a href="../models/">Saved fitted models</a> · <a href="../features.json">Exact feature whitelist</a> · <a href="../PROTOCOL.json">Protocol</a></p></body></html>'''
